{"name":"Queen’s Honour","hex":"#8b5776","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"queen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The wife, consort, or widow of a king.","A female monarch."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To act the part of a queen; to behave imperiously; to queen it.","To make a queen or (figuratively) to give the status of a queen."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/queen"},{"term":"honour","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["British, Canadian, Commonwealth, and Ireland standard spelling of honor."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["British, Canadian, Commonwealth, and Ireland standard spelling of honor."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/honour"}],"entries":[{"title":"Honour","blocks":[{"text":"Honour or honor is a quality of a person that is of both social teaching and personal ethos, that manifests itself as a code of conduct, and has various elements such as valour, chivalry, honesty, and compassion. It is an abstract concept entailing a perceived quality of worthiness and respectability that affects both the social standing and the self-evaluation of an individual or of institutions such as a family, school, regiment, or nation. Accordingly, individuals are assigned worth and stature based on the harmony of their actions with a specific code of honour, and with the moral code of the society at large."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Hamilton-burr-duel.jpg/330px-Hamilton-burr-duel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honour","wikidata":"Q207333"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Germaine_Tillion%2C_Genevi%C3%A8ve_de_Gaulle-Anthonioz%2C_Pierre_Brossolette_et_Jean_Zay_rentrent_au_Panth%C3%A9on_15.jpg/500px-Germaine_Tillion%2C_Genevi%C3%A8ve_de_Gaulle-Anthonioz%2C_Pierre_Brossolette_et_Jean_Zay_rentrent_au_Panth%C3%A9on_15.jpg?utm_so